Review the entire object and existing-concept catalog. Decide which explanatory questions warrant a new concept and which existing concept should receive a coherent update. If there are no old concepts, proceed without searching for absent history. Start from titles and descriptions, then read full relevant pages to resolve uncertain mechanisms, scope, comparisons, component-to-system relationships and update targets.

Plan concept-centrically with the concept matrix of Webster and Watson: build the object-centric table first, then transpose it. Work in this order. First, read every object page completely. The catalog does not show which ideas an object uses, and a plan that covers only the objects read so far is rejected. Second, write the object-centric table to /work/matrix.md: one line for each object alias in the catalog, listing every idea that the object uses, studies or contradicts - the problems it addresses, the ways it solves them, and the ways its results are judged - each under the name the field uses. Third, transpose the table: write one line for each idea that two or more objects list, with the aliases of those objects, and merge different names for the same idea. Keep each idea of one kind: a problem, a way of solving a problem, or a way of judging a result. Do not join ideas of different kinds in one line. An idea that a large part of the catalog shares is a theme: divide it into narrower ideas only where two or more objects still share each narrower idea, and keep the other objects under the idea that they share. Do not divide an idea down to single objects; the different ways in which objects realize one idea are what its concept explains. An idea that only one object lists stays in that object. Fourth, write result.json only when every object has a disposition. Each idea that survives becomes one concept job. Its question asks about that one idea, and its scope states the inclusion criterion that decides which objects are instances. An object that supports no surviving idea is object_only. The table is a working file, not an output.

Plan by explanatory purpose. An object may support multiple concept jobs. A job may have one supporting object only when that object states the idea in general terms or other objects need the idea to be understood. An object that already supports a shared idea needs no further job for its own features. Do not impose a concept count or force every object into a concept. Prefer an appropriate old-page update to a competing new page; one old target has at most one writer. Each job proposes one coherent research question with supporting object refs and factual scope. Avoid disconnected umbrellas, repeated questions and source-summary lists.

Every object must have an explicit disposition: it supports at least one concept_jobs entry, or it appears once in object_only. A supporting object cannot also be object_only. Assigned objects will be read completely by their Writer. Before placing an object in object_only, use native read to read its full body, continuing with offset until complete. Compare plausible counterparts or existing concepts by actual content before claiming that no useful comparison exists. If you list a compared_with reference, read its full body too. Do not mark a body as read merely because its title or description was inspected.

An object's existing factual record is not by itself a reason to exclude it from conceptual explanation. After full reading, ask whether its mechanism, conditions, failures, component/system role or relation to other approaches supports a useful explanation. A named system, a single study or details already stated in an object can still be an instance of a shared idea. Preserve useful differences rather than requiring every supporting object to share one mechanism. Do not treat uncertainty at catalog level as evidence that no concept is possible: read the pages that settle the boundary.

For object_only, give a specific content reason: what potential explanation or comparison was examined, and why it provides no additional useful explanation in this compilation. Do not use generic reasons such as 'independent object', 'object records suffice' or 'not in the chosen questions'. compared_with may be empty only when no plausible counterpart is found; then explain the candidate search scope and why the fully read object's own content does not support a standalone explanation. Do not choose an unrelated counterpart just to fill the field. This field records an explicit decision, not deletion of the object or its evidence.

Write exactly:
{"concept_jobs":[{"question":"...","scope":"...","page_refs":["P1"],"target_ref":null}],"object_only":[{"page_ref":"P2","compared_with":["P1"],"reason":"Concrete content-based decision after reading"}]}

page_refs and object_only.page_ref use only object P aliases. target_ref is an existing concept P alias or null. compared_with may use other object or existing-concept aliases, never the object itself. Both top-level lists are required and may be empty. Their union covers the entire object catalog. Do not include reviewed_objects, Topics, Cue lists or durable IDs. Write user-facing questions, scopes and reasons in output_language. Do not prewrite concept prose.
